    "excerpt": "Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) is a blood marker of ovarian follicle activity used in fertility assessment and treatment planning. It does not measure egg quality or predict an individual pregnancy.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) is a blood marker of ovarian follicle activity used in fertility assessment and treatment planning. It does not measure egg quality or predict an individual pregnancy. Immunoassays commonly measure serum AMH; QXL must confirm its platform and report time. Ask about collection at Kengeri, Yelahanka or an eligible Bengaluru home address before booking.\n\n**Also known as:** AMH, anti Mullerian hormone, Müllerian inhibiting hormone, ovarian reserve test\n\n## What is the AMH (Anti-Müllerian Hormone) test used for?\n\n- Fertility specialist assessment before assisted reproduction.\n- Selected investigation of ovarian dysfunction alongside history, ultrasound and other hormones.\n- Specialist investigation of disorders of sex development or selected ovarian tumours when specifically indicated.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Infertility\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **AMH:** Supports assessment of ovarian reserve and likely ovarian stimulation response.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nAMH is commonly quantified with a calibrated antibody-based immunoassay. Different platforms are not interchangeable. QXL assay, analyser, calibration and accredited scope remain to be confirmed.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Fasting is usually unnecessary for AMH alone; confirm if other tests are included.\n- A specific cycle day is generally not needed for AMH, but follow the fertility clinic’s schedule.\n- Disclose hormonal contraception, fertility treatment, ovarian surgery and biotin-containing supplements; do not stop treatment yourself.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A lower result may suggest fewer recruitable follicles; age and the clinical question matter.\n- Higher AMH may accompany a greater follicle count, including in PCOS; AMH alone does not establish PCOS.\n- A low result does not mean pregnancy is impossible, and a high result does not guarantee conception.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- AMH estimates a component of ovarian reserve, not a direct count of all remaining eggs.\n- Age, method, hormonal treatment and clinical context influence interpretation.\n- Do not choose treatment or predict menopause from one isolated AMH value.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- AMH complements antral follicle count and, when indicated, cycle-timed FSH/estradiol; it cannot replace an infertility evaluation.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does low AMH mean I cannot become pregnant?\n\nNo. It informs ovarian reserve assessment but cannot independently predict natural conception.\n\n### Can AMH diagnose PCOS?\n\nIt may contribute to an adult assessment in an appropriate pathway; do not diagnose PCOS from AMH alone.\n\n### Which day of my cycle is suitable?\n\nAMH is often collected on any cycle day. Use the timing requested by your fertility team.\n\n### Do I need to fast?\n\nUsually not for AMH alone. Other tests on the same request may have different preparation.\n\n### What is a normal AMH for my age?\n\nUse the method- and age-appropriate context on your report and your fertility specialist’s assessment.\n\n### Are AMH results from different laboratories comparable?\n\nPlatforms can differ. For follow-up, discuss use of the same method with your clinician.\n\n### What is QXL’s AMH price and report time?\n\nRequest the current quote and test-specific turnaround directly from QXL before booking.\n\n### Can I request home collection in Bengaluru?\n\nAsk QXL to confirm AMH collection eligibility, your pincode and whether the visit is free.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Total & Free Testosterone Test in Bengaluru](/blog/testosterone-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [Prolactin Test in Bengaluru](/blog/prolactin-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [DHEA-S Test in Bengaluru](/blog/dhea-s-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz ninth edition: reproductive chapter, PDF page2408, ovarian reserve/AMH discussion.\n- ACOG provides the caution on using AMH to predict conception in people not seeking fertility care.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
