    "excerpt": "Testosterone testing measures total hormone, its unbound fraction, or both to investigate androgen excess or deficiency. Collection timing and the free-testosterone method matter.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Testosterone testing measures total hormone, its unbound fraction, or both to investigate androgen excess or deficiency. Collection timing and the free-testosterone method matter. Serum total testosterone may be measured by immunoassay or mass spectrometry; QXL must confirm the actual method and turnaround. Arrange appropriately timed sampling through Kengeri, Yelahanka or eligible Bengaluru home collection after discussing the request.\n\n**Also known as:** serum testosterone, total testosterone, free testosterone, androgen test\n\n## What is the Testosterone — Total / Free test used for?\n\n- Symptoms suggestive of hypogonadism with an appropriate clinical evaluation.\n- Hirsutism, virilisation, menstrual disturbance or suspected androgen excess.\n- Monitoring selected hormone therapy under a treating clinician.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Hypogonadism, Androgen excess\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Total testosterone:** Includes protein-bound and unbound testosterone.\n- **Free testosterone:** Unbound hormone, measured or calculated using a validated approach; specify which.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nTotal testosterone can be measured by immunoassay or LC-MS/MS. Free testosterone may require equilibrium dialysis or a validated calculation from total testosterone, SHBG and albumin. A direct free-hormone immunoassay is not equivalent to these approaches. QXL must identify the method actually offered.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- For suspected adult male testosterone deficiency, arrange an early-morning sample and follow the clinician’s fasting instructions.\n- A low result often needs confirmation on a separate morning after recovery from acute illness.\n- Record testosterone therapy, steroid use, supplements and collection timing relative to the dose.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- Low total testosterone requires symptoms, repeat testing and possible LH/FSH assessment; one value does not establish hypogonadism.\n- SHBG changes can alter total testosterone without the same change in free testosterone.\n- High results require review of sex, age, medication exposure and clinical signs before further adrenal or ovarian investigation.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Sleep pattern, acute illness, time of day and treatment affect results.\n- Low-concentration measurement and free-testosterone methodology are important analytical limitations.\n- Do not self-start testosterone based on a laboratory number.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Total and free testosterone answer related but different questions. SHBG and albumin may be needed to resolve discordance.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Should testosterone be tested in the morning?\n\nFor investigating adult male deficiency, morning collection is usually preferred. Shift workers need an individual plan.\n\n### Are total and free testosterone the same?\n\nNo. Total includes bound hormone; free testosterone represents the unbound fraction.\n\n### Can one low value diagnose deficiency?\n\nNo. Symptoms and appropriately timed repeat testing are normally required.\n\n### Why might SHBG be requested?\n\nIt helps explain altered hormone binding and may support a validated free-testosterone calculation.\n\n### Does QXL use LC-MS/MS?\n\nThe actual QXL assay is awaiting confirmation; this page does not claim a particular platform.\n\n### Should I stop hormone treatment before testing?\n\nDo not stop treatment yourself. Ask the prescriber when to sample relative to your dose.\n\n### What is the price of total and free testosterone?\n\nAsk QXL for separate or combined prices, included parameters and test-specific turnaround.\n\n### Can I book home collection?\n\nAsk QXL to confirm an appropriate morning slot, serviceable Bengaluru pincode and collection charges.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Prolactin Test in Bengaluru](/blog/prolactin-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [DHEA-S Test in Bengaluru](/blog/dhea-s-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz PDF pages2356 and2413–2415: binding, circadian variation and free-testosterone analytical approaches.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
