    "excerpt": "Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is a blood marker used in selected prostate assessments and cancer follow-up. Total and free PSA are related measurements, not a cancer diagnosis.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is a blood marker used in selected prostate assessments and cancer follow-up. Total and free PSA are related measurements, not a cancer diagnosis. Immunoassay results need clinical interpretation and may be affected by benign prostate conditions. QXL must confirm its method and turnaround; enquire at Kengeri, Yelahanka or about eligible Bengaluru home collection and current pricing.\n\n**Also known as:** prostate specific antigen, total PSA, free PSA, percent free PSA\n\n## What is the PSA — Total / Free test used for?\n\n- Shared decision-making about prostate cancer screening based on individual risk and preferences.\n- Investigation of selected prostate symptoms with a clinician.\n- Monitoring an established prostate cancer treatment pathway.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Prostate cancer\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Total PSA:** Measures the total circulating prostate-specific antigen.\n- **Free PSA / percent free PSA:** May refine risk assessment in selected patients with an intermediate total PSA; request explicitly.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nTotal and free PSA are usually measured by separate immunometric assays. Percent free PSA is free/total PSA ×100 when appropriate. QXL must confirm the assay pairing, calculation policy and the range in which the ratio is clinically validated.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary for PSA alone.\n- Tell the clinician about urinary infection, retention, recent biopsy, catheterisation or prostate procedures; these may change timing.\n- Ask about avoiding ejaculation before collection according to the local protocol. Report finasteride/dutasteride and other treatment.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- Raised PSA can occur with benign enlargement, inflammation or cancer; it does not identify the cause.\n- A result within the report interval cannot exclude prostate cancer.\n- The need for repeat PSA, imaging or biopsy is a urological decision. Screening should include discussion of overdiagnosis and potential harms.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Treatment and prostate procedures can alter PSA substantially.\n- Percent free PSA is not a universal screening replacement or an automatic biopsy rule.\n- Serial values should ideally use a consistent assay.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- PSA complements clinical assessment and, when indicated, MRI or tissue diagnosis. A free/total ratio has a narrower role than total PSA.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does high PSA mean cancer?\n\nNo. Benign enlargement, infection or inflammation can raise it; a clinician decides the next investigation.\n\n### Can a normal PSA exclude cancer?\n\nNo. Persistent symptoms or clinical suspicion still require assessment.\n\n### Why request free PSA?\n\nA free/total ratio can help selected risk assessments; its use depends on total PSA and the clinical pathway.\n\n### Is PSA screening suitable for everyone?\n\nDiscuss individual risk, life expectancy, preferences and the benefits and harms with your doctor.\n\n### Can a urinary infection affect PSA?\n\nYes. Tell your clinician, who may defer sampling or repeat the test after recovery.\n\n### Must I fast for PSA?\n\nUsually no, unless another ordered test requires fasting.\n\n### How much are total and free PSA at QXL?\n\nAsk for a current itemised quote and whether the ratio is included.\n\n### Can QXL collect the PSA sample at home?\n\nAsk about Bengaluru pincode coverage, current free-collection conditions and report time.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [CA 19-9 Test in Bengaluru](/blog/ca19-9-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [CEA Test in Bengaluru](/blog/cea-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [AFP Test in Bengaluru](/blog/afp-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz PDF pages1107–1110: PSA, screening decisions, benign elevations and percent free PSA.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
