    "excerpt": "Prolactin is a pituitary hormone measured in blood when menstrual, fertility, lactation or selected pituitary symptoms need investigation. Stress, medicines and macroprolactin can affect interpretation.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Prolactin is a pituitary hormone measured in blood when menstrual, fertility, lactation or selected pituitary symptoms need investigation. Stress, medicines and macroprolactin can affect interpretation. Serum prolactin is commonly measured by immunoassay; QXL must confirm the assay and report time. Plan sampling with Kengeri, Yelahanka or eligible Bengaluru home collection so timing and preparation match the clinical request.\n\n**Also known as:** PRL, prolactin blood test, serum prolactin\n\n## What is the Prolactin test used for?\n\n- Unexpected breast milk production, menstrual disturbance or infertility.\n- Selected erectile, libido or pituitary symptoms assessed by a clinician.\n- Follow-up of a known prolactinoma under specialist care.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Hyperprolactinaemia\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Prolactin:** Pituitary hormone involved in lactation; assess in context.\n- **Macroprolactin assessment if indicated:** Separate follow-up investigation for some persistently raised or clinically discordant results, not automatically included.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nProlactin is commonly quantified using a sandwich immunoassay. Macroprolactin screening and dilution studies for suspected high-dose hook effect are separate laboratory investigations when clinically indicated. QXL must confirm reflex policies.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Arrange a morning collection after waking according to the clinician’s instructions, with a period of rest before sampling.\n- Avoid strenuous exercise and breast/nipple stimulation before the draw as instructed.\n- Disclose pregnancy, breastfeeding, antipsychotics, antiemetics and supplements. Confirm fasting locally; never stop prescribed medicines yourself.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- Mild elevations may require repeat collection under quieter conditions.\n- Pregnancy, breastfeeding, medicines, hypothyroidism and renal disease may explain an elevated result.\n- Persistent elevation without a clear clinical explanation may need macroprolactin assessment and endocrine review.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Raised prolactin alone does not diagnose a pituitary tumour.\n- Macroprolactin and immunoassay interference can produce misleading concentrations.\n- New severe headache or visual disturbance warrants prompt medical assessment, not routine test booking.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- TSH, pregnancy testing and other pituitary hormones may be selected after clinical assessment; they are not automatically part of a prolactin order.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Can stress raise prolactin?\n\nYes. Rested repeat sampling may clarify a mild elevation.\n\n### Do I need to fast?\n\nPreparation varies. Confirm the requested timing and fasting instructions before collection.\n\n### Do medicines affect prolactin?\n\nSome do. Give the clinician a full list and do not discontinue medicines yourself.\n\n### Does raised prolactin mean a brain tumour?\n\nNo. Several physiological, medication-related and medical causes need to be considered.\n\n### What is macroprolactin?\n\nA larger circulating form that can raise measured prolactin without the expected symptoms; it may need a separate test.\n\n### Is prolactin normally higher during breastfeeding?\n\nYes. Pregnancy and lactation require a different interpretation.\n\n### What does the QXL quote include?\n\nConfirm whether it covers prolactin alone, macroprolactin follow-up and any report discussion.\n\n### Can I arrange a home sample?\n\nAsk QXL for an appropriate morning slot, local coverage and the confirmed report time.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Total & Free Testosterone Test in Bengaluru](/blog/testosterone-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [Cortisol Test in Bengaluru](/blog/cortisol-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz PDF pages2203–2205 and2409: stress, macroprolactin, repeat sampling and differential diagnosis.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
