    "excerpt": "Cortisol testing investigates adrenal hormone production using a carefully timed specimen. Blood cortisol, late-night saliva and urinary free cortisol answer different questions and are not interchangeable.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Cortisol testing investigates adrenal hormone production using a carefully timed specimen. Blood cortisol, late-night saliva and urinary free cortisol answer different questions and are not interchangeable. Serum assays commonly use immunoassay or mass spectrometry; confirm QXL’s offered specimen, method and turnaround. Kengeri, Yelahanka and Bengaluru home collection require a timing plan before this test is booked.\n\n**Also known as:** serum cortisol, morning cortisol, hydrocortisone, adrenal function test\n\n## What is the Cortisol test used for?\n\n- Clinician-directed evaluation of suspected adrenal insufficiency.\n- A selected Cushing syndrome investigation using a validated testing pathway.\n- Monitoring an established adrenal or pituitary disorder.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Adrenal insufficiency, Cushing syndrome\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Cortisol:** Timed serum cortisol is the main focus; other matrices require separate requests.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nCortisol can be measured by immunoassay or LC-MS/MS. Total serum cortisol differs from free cortisol in urine or saliva. QXL must specify the matrix, assay and protocol; a random blood value is not a universal screening test for Cushing syndrome.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Record exact collection time and follow the clinician’s morning or protocol-specific timing.\n- Disclose oral, inhaled, topical and injected steroids, oestrogen therapy, sleep patterns and recent illness.\n- Do not stop glucocorticoids abruptly or self-administer dexamethasone; stimulation and suppression tests require medical direction.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- Interpret blood values against time-specific, assay-specific limits.\n- Abnormal basal cortisol may require ACTH measurement or a supervised dynamic test.\n- Acute stress and altered cortisol-binding proteins can affect total cortisol.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- One random value cannot reliably diagnose all adrenal disorders.\n- Steroid medicines may alter physiology or cross-react in some assays.\n- Severe weakness with fainting, persistent vomiting or suspected adrenal crisis needs emergency medical care.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Morning serum cortisol, late-night salivary cortisol, urinary free cortisol and dexamethasone suppression testing serve different clinical purposes.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why does the collection time matter?\n\nCortisol follows a daily rhythm; a morning and an evening value use different clinical expectations.\n\n### Is a random cortisol enough for Cushing syndrome?\n\nUsually not. A clinician selects a validated screening pathway and interprets confirmatory results.\n\n### Can steroid medicines affect cortisol?\n\nYes, through physiological suppression or assay interference. Never stop prescribed steroids abruptly.\n\n### Is serum cortisol the same as urinary free cortisol?\n\nNo. The specimen and the hormone fraction measured differ.\n\n### Do I need stimulation testing?\n\nOnly your clinician can decide after the clinical assessment and baseline results.\n\n### Should illness be mentioned?\n\nYes. Acute illness and stress may affect cortisol and the reason for testing.\n\n### What is QXL’s cortisol price and turnaround?\n\nConfirm the exact specimen and protocol first, then request the current quote and report time.\n\n### Can a home appointment meet the required time?\n\nAsk QXL to confirm an appropriate time slot and transport window before booking.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Total & Free Testosterone Test in Bengaluru](/blog/testosterone-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [DHEA-S Test in Bengaluru](/blog/dhea-s-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Manual ninth edition, PDF pages395–397 (printed379–381): timing, medication effects and dynamic testing; older numerical cutoffs are not copied.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
