    "excerpt": "D-dimer detects fragments released when cross-linked fibrin is broken down. Clinicians use it with a validated clinical probability assessment when investigating possible venous thrombosis, or within a broader coagulation work-up.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** D-dimer detects fragments released when cross-linked fibrin is broken down. Clinicians use it with a validated clinical probability assessment when investigating possible venous thrombosis, or within a broader coagulation work-up. Citrated plasma immunoassay methods and units vary. QXL must confirm its method and report time; urgent symptoms require medical care rather than waiting for Bengaluru home collection.\n\n**Also known as:** D dimer, fibrin degradation marker, D-dimer FEU, D-dimer DDU\n\n## What is the D-Dimer test used for?\n\n- Rule-out pathways for suspected DVT or pulmonary embolism in appropriately selected patients.\n- Assessment of disseminated intravascular coagulation alongside platelet count, PT and fibrinogen.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Venous thromboembolism, Disseminated intravascular coagulation\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **D-dimer:** Quantitative or qualitative fibrin-breakdown marker; units and assay threshold must be specified.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nQuantitative immunoturbidimetric or other immunoassays may be used. QXL must identify assay sensitivity, analytical range, units (FEU versus DDU) and the validated clinical cutoff. Do not transfer a threshold between methods.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Citrated venous plasma is commonly required. Confirm QXL tube, fill requirement and processing deadline.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary.\n- Disclose anticoagulants, recent surgery, pregnancy, infection and the time symptoms began.\n- Use a correctly filled citrate tube and the validated transport pathway; underfilling or clotting may invalidate the sample.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A negative result can help exclude VTE only within an appropriate clinical probability pathway using a validated assay.\n- A positive result does not locate a clot or establish DVT/PE; imaging may be needed.\n- Pregnancy, age, inflammation, malignancy and recent procedures can raise D-dimer without acute VTE.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Not suitable as a general wellness screen.\n- Anticoagulation and delayed sampling can affect sensitivity.\n- Chest pain, sudden breathlessness, collapse or coughing blood requires immediate medical assessment; do not wait for a home blood draw.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- D-dimer is not a replacement for ultrasound or chest imaging when clinical probability warrants it.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does a positive D-dimer prove a blood clot?\n\nNo. It is nonspecific; clinical assessment determines whether imaging is needed.\n\n### Can a negative result rule out pulmonary embolism?\n\nOnly in a suitable low/non-high probability pathway with an appropriate assay. High suspicion needs assessment regardless.\n\n### What are FEU and DDU?\n\nThey are different reporting conventions. Read the units and method-specific cutoff rather than comparing raw numbers.\n\n### Why is D-dimer higher during pregnancy?\n\nPregnancy changes coagulation physiology; interpretation requires a pregnancy-appropriate clinical pathway.\n\n### Can I fast before the sample?\n\nFasting is generally not needed for D-dimer itself.\n\n### Should I book a home test for sudden chest pain?\n\nNo. Seek immediate medical care; routine collection is not an emergency diagnostic pathway.\n\n### Does QXL provide urgent D-dimer reporting?\n\nUrgent capability and expected report time must be confirmed directly with QXL.\n\n### Which doctor reviews the result?\n\nA qualified laboratory specialist authorises the report; the treating clinician assesses clot risk and next steps.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [High-Sensitivity Troponin Test in Bengaluru](/blog/high-sensitivity-cardiac-troponin-bengaluru-guide)\n- [NT-proBNP Test in Bengaluru](/blog/nt-probnp-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Laposata PDF pages101–105: DIC interpretation and assay variation.\n- Manual PDF pages183–185: D-dimer, FEU/DDU and preanalytical context.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
