    "excerpt": "ANA by indirect immunofluorescence detects antinuclear antibodies and describes their titre and staining pattern. It can support a connective tissue disease evaluation when symptoms justify testing, but a positive result alone does not diagnose lupus.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** ANA by indirect immunofluorescence detects antinuclear antibodies and describes their titre and staining pattern. It can support a connective tissue disease evaluation when symptoms justify testing, but a positive result alone does not diagnose lupus. Serum is commonly examined on HEp-2 cells. QXL must confirm substrate, dilution policy and turnaround; enquire through Kengeri, Yelahanka or eligible Bengaluru home collection.\n\n**Also known as:** antinuclear antibody, ANA HEp-2 IFA, FANA, ANA immunofluorescence\n\n## What is the ANA by IFA test used for?\n\n- Clinical suspicion of lupus, systemic sclerosis, Sjögren disease or related systemic autoimmune disease.\n- A specialist-selected investigation after symptoms, examination and organ-specific findings.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Systemic lupus erythematosus, Systemic sclerosis, Sjögren disease\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **ANA titre:** Highest positive dilution in the laboratory’s reporting protocol.\n- **ANA pattern:** Fluorescence pattern used to guide clinically appropriate follow-up.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nIndirect immunofluorescence on an appropriate cell substrate, often HEp-2, assesses antibody staining at defined dilutions. QXL must confirm substrate, screening dilution, endpoint titration and pattern nomenclature. IFA is not interchangeable with an ANA ELISA screen.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Fasting is usually not needed for ANA alone.\n- Provide relevant symptoms, previous ANA method/results and medicines associated with autoantibodies.\n- Do not discontinue medication solely for this test without prescriber guidance.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A positive result is a finding that must be interpreted with titre, pattern and pretest probability.\n- Low-level positivity can occur in healthy people, with infection or with certain medicines.\n- ENA or anti-dsDNA tests should be selected according to clinical features and ANA findings, rather than ordered automatically for every positive result.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- ANA is not a general explanation for unexplained fatigue or a stand-alone disease diagnosis.\n- Negative ANA reduces the likelihood of some conditions but cannot exclude every autoimmune disorder.\n- Serial ANA titres are usually not a useful measure of lupus disease activity.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- ANA IFA shows patterns and titres; antigen-specific ENA/anti-dsDNA assays answer different questions. Anti-CCP is more relevant to suspected rheumatoid arthritis.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does positive ANA mean lupus?\n\nNo. Symptoms, titre, pattern and other clinical findings determine its significance.\n\n### What does the titre mean?\n\nIt records the dilution at which staining remains detectable using the laboratory’s protocol.\n\n### Why does the pattern matter?\n\nIt can guide further testing, but a pattern alone cannot establish a diagnosis.\n\n### Is ANA IFA different from ANA ELISA?\n\nYes. They use different analytical approaches and may not detect the same antibody spectrum.\n\n### Can healthy people have positive ANA?\n\nYes, especially at lower titres; clinical context is essential.\n\n### Should I repeat ANA to monitor treatment?\n\nUsually not simply to track disease activity. Your clinician chooses appropriate follow-up markers.\n\n### Does QXL’s price include titre and pattern?\n\nAsk for confirmation of the method, titration/reflex policy, included reporting and current quote.\n\n### Can I request home collection?\n\nAsk QXL to confirm Bengaluru serviceability, whether collection is free and the test-specific report time.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [HLA-B27 Test in Bengaluru](/blog/hla-b27-bengaluru-guide)\n- [Anti-CCP Antibody Test in Bengaluru](/blog/anti-ccp-antibody-bengaluru-guide)\n- [Anti-TPO Antibody Test in Bengaluru](/blog/anti-tpo-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Laposata PDF pages143–145 (printed131–133): IFA titre/pattern and clinical interpretation.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
