    "excerpt": "Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody (anti-TPO) testing looks for evidence of thyroid autoimmunity in blood. It helps explain selected abnormal thyroid function findings but does not measure thyroid hormone production.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody (anti-TPO) testing looks for evidence of thyroid autoimmunity in blood. It helps explain selected abnormal thyroid function findings but does not measure thyroid hormone production. Immunoassay cutoffs vary between methods. QXL must confirm its assay and turnaround; enquire at Kengeri, Yelahanka or about eligible Bengaluru home collection, and interpret results alongside TSH and free T4.\n\n**Also known as:** thyroid peroxidase antibody, TPOAb, anti microsomal antibody, thyroid autoantibody\n\n## What is the Anti-TPO Antibody test used for?\n\n- Investigating possible autoimmune thyroiditis after thyroid function assessment.\n- Selected pregnancy-related thyroid evaluation or risk assessment under clinician guidance.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Autoimmune thyroiditis\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Anti-TPO antibody:** Marker supporting assessment of autoimmune thyroid disease.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nAn immunoassay detects antibodies to thyroid peroxidase. Calibration and thresholds differ across manufacturers. QXL assay, reporting units and positivity cutoff must be verified before publication of numerical limits.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary for anti-TPO alone.\n- Tell the clinician about thyroid treatment, pregnancy and biotin-containing supplements.\n- Continue prescribed therapy unless the treating clinician gives different instructions.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A positive result supports thyroid autoimmunity but does not tell whether hormone levels are currently high, low or normal.\n- TSH and free T4 help establish thyroid function and guide treatment.\n- A negative anti-TPO result does not exclude all causes of thyroid dysfunction.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Antibody concentration is not a treatment dose target.\n- Repeated anti-TPO measurement is often unnecessary when thyroid function monitoring answers the clinical question.\n- Do not compare numerical results from different assays as if they were interchangeable.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Anti-TPO assesses autoimmunity; TSH/free T4 assess function. TSH receptor antibodies have a different role, particularly in Graves disease.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does positive anti-TPO mean I need thyroxine?\n\nNot by itself. Treatment depends on thyroid function, symptoms, pregnancy and the clinical assessment.\n\n### Can anti-TPO be positive with normal TSH?\n\nYes. Antibodies and thyroid hormone function describe different aspects of thyroid health.\n\n### Is anti-TPO the same as TSH?\n\nNo. Anti-TPO is an antibody marker; TSH reflects regulation of thyroid hormone production.\n\n### Do I need to fast?\n\nUsually not for anti-TPO alone; check preparation for any accompanying tests.\n\n### Should I repeat antibodies frequently?\n\nOften this adds little. Discuss whether TSH/free T4 monitoring is more appropriate.\n\n### Can I compare results from different labs?\n\nUse caution because methods and positivity cutoffs vary.\n\n### What is QXL’s anti-TPO price?\n\nAsk for the current price, report time and whether other thyroid tests are included.\n\n### Is home collection available?\n\nAsk QXL to confirm eligibility, pincode coverage and whether there is a collection fee.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [ANA by IFA Test in Bengaluru](/blog/ana-ifa-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz PDF pages2317–2318: anti-TPO assay variation and limited value of serial antibody measurements.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
