    "excerpt": "CA-125 is a blood tumour marker used mainly in selected ovarian cancer management and assessment of a pelvic mass. It can rise in benign conditions and cannot reliably screen an average-risk person for ovarian cancer.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** CA-125 is a blood tumour marker used mainly in selected ovarian cancer management and assessment of a pelvic mass. It can rise in benign conditions and cannot reliably screen an average-risk person for ovarian cancer. Immunoassay methods differ. QXL must confirm its platform and turnaround; current prices and eligible Bengaluru collection through Kengeri or Yelahanka should be confirmed before booking.\n\n**Also known as:** CA125, cancer antigen125, ovarian tumour marker\n\n## What is the CA-125 test used for?\n\n- Monitoring selected known ovarian cancers under oncology care.\n- Risk assessment of a pelvic/adnexal mass alongside ultrasound and clinical findings.\n- Clinician-directed investigation of persistent symptoms when appropriate.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Ovarian cancer\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **CA-125:** Tumour-associated antigen used with clinical and imaging findings.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nCA-125 is commonly measured by an automated immunometric assay. Results from different platforms can differ. For trends, use the same laboratory/method when practical and record treatment timing.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary for CA-125 alone.\n- Tell the clinician about menstruation, pregnancy, endometriosis, recent abdominal surgery and liver disease.\n- Bring prior results and treatment dates for meaningful serial comparison.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- An elevated result is not proof of malignancy; menstruation, endometriosis and other benign processes may raise it.\n- A result within the laboratory interval does not exclude ovarian cancer.\n- Serial changes in an established cancer pathway require oncology interpretation with symptoms and imaging.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Not an average-risk population screening test.\n- An isolated value cannot determine cancer stage, treatment response or recurrence.\n- Persistent abdominal bloating, early satiety or pelvic symptoms need clinical assessment regardless of the result.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- CA-125 complements imaging and clinical risk assessment. Other tumour markers answer different questions and are not an automatic “all cancer” panel.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Can CA-125 detect every ovarian cancer?\n\nNo. Some ovarian cancers do not raise it, especially in earlier disease.\n\n### Does a high value prove cancer?\n\nNo. Several benign conditions and physiological states can cause an increase.\n\n### Should healthy people use it for routine cancer screening?\n\nIt is not recommended as a routine average-risk ovarian cancer screening test.\n\n### Can menstruation affect CA-125?\n\nYes. Tell the clinician about cycle timing when interpreting the result.\n\n### Do I need fasting?\n\nUsually no, unless another test on the request needs it.\n\n### Is a rising marker always a recurrence?\n\nNo. Confirmed trends must be interpreted with the clinical and imaging picture.\n\n### What is QXL’s CA-125 cost?\n\nRequest the current quote and report time; this draft does not publish an unverified amount.\n\n### Who can discuss my result?\n\nAsk QXL about laboratory report discussion and take the result to the treating gynaecologist or oncologist.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [CA 19-9 Test in Bengaluru](/blog/ca19-9-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [CEA Test in Bengaluru](/blog/cea-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [AFP Test in Bengaluru](/blog/afp-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz PDF pages1102–1104: CA-125 uses, screening limitations and pelvic-mass interpretation.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
