    "excerpt": "Allergen-specific IgE blood testing looks for sensitisation to selected allergens suggested by the history. A positive result does not by itself confirm clinical allergy or predict reaction severity.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Allergen-specific IgE blood testing looks for sensitisation to selected allergens suggested by the history. A positive result does not by itself confirm clinical allergy or predict reaction severity. Serum antibody assays can assess individual allergens or a defined panel. QXL must confirm the exact panel, method and turnaround; enquire about Kengeri, Yelahanka and eligible Bengaluru home collection before booking.\n\n**Also known as:** allergen specific IgE, allergy blood panel, sIgE, ImmunoCAP, RAST\n\n## What is the Allergy / Specific IgE Panels test used for?\n\n- A history suggestive of an IgE-mediated reaction to a specific exposure.\n- Allergy specialist assessment when skin testing is unsuitable or additional evidence is needed.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** IgE-mediated allergy\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Allergen-specific IgE:** Each named allergen is a separate result; confirm the exact panel list.\n- **Total IgE if ordered:** Different measurement that does not identify the causative allergen; not automatically included.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nSpecific IgE is measured by an allergen-binding immunoassay; single-allergen, multiplex and component-resolved tests differ. QXL must specify allergen extracts/components, report units, thresholds and platform. Do not substitute food IgG panels for IgE allergy assessment.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum is commonly used. QXL must confirm the validated specimen for its assay.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary.\n- Provide a detailed reaction history, suspected trigger, timing and medicines.\n- Antihistamine rules for skin tests are not automatically applicable to blood IgE testing; confirm advice and do not stop medication yourself.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Minimum volume and paediatric volume: confirm QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the QXL-approved collection tube; do not select tubes from general textbook examples. |\n| Stability | Separation deadline, validated temperature, cold-chain and transport window: confirm QXL SOP. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, volume, specimen quality and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A positive specific IgE demonstrates sensitisation; symptoms and exposure determine clinical allergy.\n- An IgE concentration or class does not reliably predict the severity of the next reaction.\n- Negative tests may not resolve a convincing reaction history; an allergist may recommend further assessment.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the interval, units and decision limits printed on the QXL report for the actual method, age and clinical context. No universal numeric range is substituted here.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Broad untargeted screening can yield clinically irrelevant positives and unnecessary dietary restriction.\n- Cross-reactivity between allergens can affect interpretation.\n- Suspected anaphylaxis requires immediate medical care rather than waiting for allergy testing.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Specific IgE, total IgE, skin-prick testing and supervised food challenge have distinct roles. Food-specific IgG is not an equivalent allergy test.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nRecord the clinical indication, relevant treatment and sampling time. Resolve unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote; confirm included parameters, collection charges and report review.\n- **Report time:** Confirm the test-specific QXL turnaround before collection, including the starting point, referral transit and working-day conditions.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL about free Bengaluru home collection for this test and your pincode. Availability, collection timing and specimen eligibility need confirmation.\n- **Processing:** Performing laboratory and in-house versus referral status: QXL verification pending.\n- **Report review:** Reports should be authorised by an appropriately qualified laboratory specialist for the discipline. Ask QXL about a separate report-discussion appointment; this page does not record medical approval.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does positive specific IgE prove an allergy?\n\nNo. It demonstrates sensitisation and must match the reaction history.\n\n### Does a higher IgE class mean a more severe reaction?\n\nNot reliably. A clinician assesses reaction risk using the whole clinical picture.\n\n### Should I request the biggest available panel?\n\nTargeted testing guided by symptoms is often more useful than indiscriminate panels.\n\n### Is food IgG the same as food IgE?\n\nNo. Food IgG panels should not be used as an equivalent test for IgE-mediated allergy.\n\n### Must I stop antihistamines?\n\nAntihistamine restrictions for skin testing do not automatically apply to blood IgE testing. Confirm the actual test instructions, and do not stop prescribed medicines on your own.\n\n### Will I need to avoid every positive food?\n\nDo not exclude tolerated foods solely from a panel result; discuss this with an allergist.\n\n### How much does a QXL allergy panel cost?\n\nRequest the exact allergen list, method, current price and turnaround before booking.\n\n### Can the sample be collected at home?\n\nAsk QXL about your pincode and collection conditions; acute breathing difficulty or collapse needs emergency care.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [ANA by IFA Test in Bengaluru](/blog/ana-ifa-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Manual PDF pages636–638 (printed620–622): single-allergen IgE and clinical context.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
