    "excerpt": "The double marker test measures maternal serum PAPP-A and free beta-hCG to estimate risk for selected fetal chromosome conditions. Combined screening adds nuchal-translucency ultrasound and maternal information.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** The double marker test measures maternal serum PAPP-A and free beta-hCG to estimate risk for selected fetal chromosome conditions. Combined screening adds nuchal-translucency ultrasound and maternal information. It provides a risk estimate rather than a diagnosis. Accurate dating and clinical details are essential, and QXL must confirm the validated blood-test and ultrasound windows before collection.\n\n**Also known as:** Dual marker, PAPP-A free beta hCG, Combined first trimester screen\n\n## What is the Double Marker Test (First-Trimester Screening) test used for?\n\n- A pregnant patient chooses first-trimester aneuploidy screening after counselling.\n- An obstetrician requests combined screening within the laboratory’s validated gestational window.\n- Clarification of screening options alongside diagnostic tests and cfDNA screening.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Fetal trisomy21, Fetal trisomy18\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **PAPP-A:** Pregnancy-associated plasma protein A; an input to gestation-adjusted risk calculation.\n- **Free beta-hCG:** A placental hormone component interpreted within the validated screening algorithm.\n- **Nuchal translucency and maternal information:** Ultrasound and clinical inputs used for combined risk assessment; they are not additional blood analytes.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nGeneral principle: quantitative immunoassays measure maternal serum PAPP-A and free beta-hCG. Validated software converts values to gestation-adjusted multiples of the median and calculates risk using maternal and, when applicable, ultrasound information. QXL must confirm its analytes, software, population adjustments and gestational window.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Maternal venous blood for serum plus a completed prenatal screening form and the required dating/NT ultrasound information. QXL must confirm the exact collection and submission requirements.\n\n- Check gestational eligibility with the obstetrician before booking; exact blood and ultrasound windows differ by validated programme.\n- Bring ultrasound dating/CRL/NT details where required, maternal date of birth, weight and relevant pregnancy history.\n- Report twins, IVF/donor conception, diabetes and previous affected pregnancies to the clinician.\n- Fasting is generally not required for the marker measurements alone.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation. |\n| Container | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation; use the approved collection kit. |\n| Stability | Pending QXL validated time/temperature and transport instructions. |\n| Rejection | QXL to confirm assay-specific rejection rules; identifier mismatch, unsuitable container or compromised sample requires laboratory review. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- The report provides a risk estimate, not a fetal diagnosis. Individual marker values should not be interpreted separately as proof of aneuploidy.\n- A higher-risk screen requires counselling and discussion of diagnostic or further screening options.\n- A lower-risk screen reduces estimated risk but does not exclude all chromosome or structural conditions.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the QXL report’s assay-specific units, reference interval or decision limit. Age, sex, pregnancy, sampling time and clinical context may change interpretation; there is no universal range for this page.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Incorrect gestational age or maternal details can distort the calculated risk.\n- Twin and assisted-conception pregnancies require programme-specific handling.\n- This screen is not a comprehensive genetic test or a substitute for fetal anomaly ultrasound.\n- Do not make irreversible pregnancy decisions based on screening alone.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Double-marker combined screening uses serum markers and ultrasound information. NIPT examines maternal-plasma cfDNA; CVS/amniocentesis are diagnostic procedures.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nProvide the diagnostic question, prior results and relevant treatment. Confirm the exact assay, specimen SOP and performing laboratory. Discuss results that conflict with the clinical picture before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Price requires QXL approval; no unverified price is offered.\n- **Report time:** QXL test-specific turnaround pending confirmation; do not infer from another laboratory.\n- **Home collection:** QXL to confirm test-specific eligibility, collection setting and transport before booking. Ask QXL whether eligible home collection in your Bengaluru pincode is free; do not assume every specimen can be collected at home.\n- **Processing:** QXL to confirm in-house versus referral processing and assay/platform.\n- **Report review:** Proposed reviewer assignment only. Medical review, operations verification and sign-off are pending.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What are the two markers?\n\nThey are PAPP-A and free beta-hCG in maternal serum. QXL must confirm the exact analytes in its validated screening programme.\n\n### When is first-trimester screening performed?\n\nIt is performed in the first trimester, commonly around 10–13 weeks for combined assessment. The obstetrician and laboratory must confirm the valid blood-test and NT-ultrasound windows for your pregnancy.\n\n### Is the blood test alone the same as combined screening?\n\nNo. Combined screening incorporates ultrasound NT and maternal data with serum results. The report should identify the inputs used.\n\n### Does a high-risk result diagnose Down syndrome?\n\nNo. It indicates increased estimated risk and needs counselling about further evaluation or diagnostic testing.\n\n### Can a low-risk result guarantee an unaffected baby?\n\nNo screening test can exclude every genetic or structural condition. Continue recommended antenatal and ultrasound care.\n\n### Why do you need my ultrasound and weight?\n\nRisk calculations depend on gestational dating and maternal factors. Inaccurate inputs can produce a misleading estimate.\n\n### Should I take this and NIPT simultaneously?\n\nChoose a coherent screening strategy with your obstetrician. Independent parallel screens can create confusing results and may be unnecessary.\n\n### Do I need to fast?\n\nFasting is generally unnecessary for the two serum markers alone. Check whether other ordered tests have different instructions.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [NIPT Test in Bengaluru](/blog/nipt-cell-free-dna-prenatal-screening-bengaluru-guide)\n- [AFP Test in Bengaluru](/blog/afp-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Clinical principles are supported by the cited textbook sections and current primary guidance; actual QXL kit, specimen volume, stability, price, turnaround and availability remain unverified.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
