    "excerpt": "NIPT analyses cell-free DNA in maternal blood to screen for selected chromosome conditions, usually including trisomies 21, 18 and 13. It can produce high-risk, low-risk or non-reportable results and does not replace diagnostic testing or recommended ultrasound.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** NIPT analyses cell-free DNA in maternal blood to screen for selected chromosome conditions, usually including trisomies 21, 18 and 13. It can produce high-risk, low-risk or non-reportable results and does not replace diagnostic testing or recommended ultrasound. Pretest counselling, pregnancy eligibility and follow-up matter. QXL must confirm the provider, approved sample kit and reporting pathway.\n\n**Also known as:** cfDNA, Non invasive prenatal screening, NIPS\n\n## What is the NIPT / Cell-Free DNA Prenatal Screening test used for?\n\n- A pregnant patient chooses cfDNA screening after pretest counselling.\n- An obstetrician considers cfDNA appropriate to the pregnancy, gestation and chosen screening pathway.\n- Discussion of follow-up options after previous screening, with counselling on benefits and limitations.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Fetal trisomy21, Fetal trisomy18, Fetal trisomy13\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Placental cell-free DNA signal:** A fraction of maternal-plasma DNA reflects placental material used to estimate risk for targeted chromosome conditions.\n- **Common trisomies 21, 18 and 13:** The core screening targets; exact assay scope and eligibility require confirmation.\n- **Fetal fraction / assay quality measures:** Quality information may determine whether a reliable result can be issued.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nGeneral principle: sequencing or another validated molecular approach analyses maternal-plasma cfDNA with bioinformatic risk assessment. Much of the pregnancy-related signal comes from placenta, not direct fetal tissue. QXL must confirm the provider, platform, scope, quality thresholds and genetic counselling pathway.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Maternal venous blood collected in the provider-approved cfDNA tube. This specialised collection/transport protocol must be confirmed; ordinary serum tubes are not interchangeable.\n\n- Discuss the choice to accept or decline screening and its possible outcomes before collection.\n- Confirm pregnancy dating, number of fetuses, viability and assay eligibility; cfDNA screening is generally possible from 10 weeks.\n- Report vanishing twin, donor conception, transplantation and relevant medical history to the obstetrician and laboratory.\n- Use the approved kit and transport window. Fasting is generally not required.\n- Arrange clinically appropriate reporting that complies with applicable Indian prenatal-testing requirements; no fetal-sex result is offered by this page.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation. |\n| Container | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation; use the approved collection kit. |\n| Stability | Pending QXL validated time/temperature and transport instructions. |\n| Rejection | QXL to confirm assay-specific rejection rules; identifier mismatch, unsuitable container or compromised sample requires laboratory review. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A high-risk screen requires genetic counselling and diagnostic confirmation when appropriate before irreversible decisions.\n- A low-risk result reduces risk for targeted conditions; it does not exclude all chromosome, genetic or structural abnormalities.\n- A no-call or uninterpretable result is not reassurance; it needs a clinician-directed follow-up plan.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the QXL report’s assay-specific units, reference interval or decision limit. Age, sex, pregnancy, sampling time and clinical context may change interpretation; there is no universal range for this page.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Placental mosaicism and other biological factors can cause discordance with fetal findings.\n- Performance and eligibility depend on the condition, assay, pregnancy and fetal fraction.\n- Routine expanded microdeletion or genome-wide screening is not assumed appropriate.\n- NIPT does not replace recommended obstetric ultrasound or diagnostic testing where indicated.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- NIPT is screening from maternal blood. CVS or amniocentesis obtains material for diagnostic testing; ultrasound assesses fetal anatomy and pregnancy findings.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nProvide the diagnostic question, prior results and relevant treatment. Confirm the exact assay, specimen SOP and performing laboratory. Discuss results that conflict with the clinical picture before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Price requires QXL approval; no unverified price is offered.\n- **Report time:** QXL test-specific turnaround pending confirmation; do not infer from another laboratory.\n- **Home collection:** QXL to confirm test-specific eligibility, collection setting and transport before booking. Ask QXL whether eligible home collection in your Bengaluru pincode is free; do not assume every specimen can be collected at home.\n- **Processing:** QXL to confirm in-house versus referral processing and assay/platform.\n- **Report review:** Proposed QXL laboratory reviewer: Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Biochemistry), approval pending. Additional qualified obstetric/genetics review and referral-provider verification remain required.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is NIPT diagnostic?\n\nNo. It estimates risk for the conditions included in the assay. A high-risk result needs counselling and consideration of confirmatory diagnostic testing.\n\n### When can it be done?\n\nMany cfDNA programmes accept samples from 10 weeks of pregnancy. Eligibility and the approved gestational window must be confirmed for the actual assay.\n\n### Does it test the baby’s cells directly?\n\nIt analyses cell-free DNA in maternal plasma; the pregnancy-related signal is mainly placental. This explains some possible discordant results.\n\n### What does no-call mean?\n\nThe laboratory could not issue an interpretable screening result. Your clinician should discuss further evaluation, which may include ultrasound and diagnostic testing.\n\n### Does a low-risk result replace ultrasound?\n\nNo. Structural abnormalities may occur despite low-risk chromosome screening, so recommended ultrasound care continues.\n\n### Should I choose the largest expanded panel?\n\nMore targets do not automatically improve screening. Discuss what is validated, the chance of uncertain findings and whether diagnostic testing better addresses your question.\n\n### Is a special blood tube needed?\n\nUsually a provider-specific cfDNA kit is required. The collection team must follow the approved tube, fill and transport instructions.\n\n### Who should interpret my result?\n\nAn obstetric or genetics professional should explain the result and follow-up. QXL laboratory reviewer and referral-provider details remain to be assigned and approved.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Double Marker Test in Bengaluru](/blog/double-marker-first-trimester-screening-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Clinical principles are supported by the cited textbook sections and current primary guidance; actual QXL kit, specimen volume, stability, price, turnaround and availability remain unverified.\n- Proposed laboratory review: Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Biochemistry), pending. Additional qualified obstetric/genetics review and referral-laboratory approval are required; no genetics qualification or completed specialist review is claimed.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
