    "excerpt": "H. pylori stool antigen testing can help identify active Helicobacter pylori infection and, with suitable timing, assess eradication after treatment.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** H. pylori stool antigen testing can help identify active Helicobacter pylori infection and, with suitable timing, assess eradication after treatment. Recent acid-suppressing medicines, antibiotics or bismuth can produce a false-negative result. Medication timing must be agreed with the treating clinician. QXL must confirm its assay, container and transport instructions before a Bengaluru collection is arranged.\n\n**Also known as:** HpSA, Helicobacter pylori antigen stool\n\n## What is the H. pylori Stool Antigen test used for?\n\n- Clinician-directed testing for suspected H. pylori infection in an appropriate dyspepsia or ulcer assessment.\n- Test of cure after an eradication-treatment course, timed by the clinician.\n- A noninvasive active-infection test when suitable to the clinical pathway.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Helicobacter pylori infection\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **H. pylori stool antigen:** Bacterial antigen detected in a stool sample; distinct from a blood antibody response.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nGeneral principle: an immunoassay uses antibodies to detect H. pylori antigens in stool. Formats and validation differ. QXL must confirm the actual kit, whether it is validated for eradication assessment and its assay-specific collection rules.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** A stool sample in a clean, approved, leakproof container without urine, toilet-water or disinfectant contamination. QXL must specify the required amount, preservatives, storage and transport time.\n\n- Tell the clinician about PPIs/PCABs, antibiotics and bismuth before collection; arrange medication timing under medical direction.\n- For test of cure, current ACG guidance recommends testing at least four weeks after therapy and avoiding PPIs for two weeks and antibiotics/bismuth for four weeks to reduce false negatives.\n- Do not stop necessary treatment independently; the clinician must balance test accuracy and your symptoms.\n- Fasting is generally not required for the stool antigen assay.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation. |\n| Container | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation; use the approved collection kit. |\n| Stability | Pending QXL validated time/temperature and transport instructions. |\n| Rejection | QXL to confirm assay-specific rejection rules; identifier mismatch, unsuitable container or compromised sample requires laboratory review. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A positive result supports active H. pylori infection in the appropriate context.\n- A negative result can be unreliable when testing occurs too soon after treatment or during suppressive medicine use.\n- Persistent symptoms require clinical reassessment even after a negative result; this test does not assess all causes of abdominal pain.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the QXL report’s assay-specific units, reference interval or decision limit. Age, sex, pregnancy, sampling time and clinical context may change interpretation; there is no universal range for this page.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Incorrect timing, medication exposure or specimen handling can reduce detection.\n- It does not identify an ulcer, cancer or antibiotic susceptibility by itself.\n- A blood antibody test is not an interchangeable test of cure because antibodies can persist.\n- Bleeding, unexplained weight loss, persistent vomiting or other concerning symptoms require medical assessment.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Stool antigen and urea breath testing assess active infection. Serology measures antibodies and cannot reliably confirm eradication.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nProvide the diagnostic question, prior results and relevant treatment. Confirm the exact assay, specimen SOP and performing laboratory. Discuss results that conflict with the clinical picture before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Price requires QXL approval; no unverified price is offered.\n- **Report time:** QXL test-specific turnaround pending confirmation; do not infer from another laboratory.\n- **Home collection:** QXL to confirm test-specific eligibility, collection setting and transport before booking. Ask QXL whether eligible home collection in your Bengaluru pincode is free; do not assume every specimen can be collected at home.\n- **Processing:** QXL to confirm in-house versus referral processing and assay/platform.\n- **Report review:** Proposed reviewer assignment only. Medical review, operations verification and sign-off are pending.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does this test detect active infection?\n\nIt detects H. pylori antigen in stool and is used as a noninvasive active-infection test when clinically appropriate.\n\n### Can it check whether treatment worked?\n\nYes, if the assay is validated for that purpose and the sample is collected after the recommended treatment and medicine intervals.\n\n### Why do PPIs and antibiotics matter?\n\nThey can suppress the organism and produce a false-negative result. Plan any withholding period with the treating clinician.\n\n### When should a test of cure be collected?\n\nThe ACG recommends at least four weeks after therapy, with two weeks without a PPI and four weeks without antibiotics or bismuth. Confirm an individual plan with your clinician.\n\n### Can I use a blood antibody result instead?\n\nAntibodies may remain after infection clears, so antibody testing is not a suitable substitute for confirming eradication.\n\n### How should I collect the stool?\n\nUse the approved container and avoid urine or toilet-water contamination. Obtain QXL’s exact amount, temperature and delivery instructions first.\n\n### Do I need to fast?\n\nFasting is generally unnecessary for stool antigen testing. Medication timing is usually the more important preparation issue.\n\n### What if I still have pain after a negative result?\n\nSeek clinical review. Other disorders or a false-negative test may need consideration, especially if preparation was unsuitable or symptoms are concerning.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [HIV 4th Generation Test in Bengaluru](/blog/hiv-1-2-antigen-antibody-4th-generation-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Clinical principles are supported by the cited textbook sections and current primary guidance; actual QXL kit, specimen volume, stability, price, turnaround and availability remain unverified.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
