    "excerpt": "Intact PTH is a blood measurement used to assess calcium imbalance, parathyroid function and selected kidney or bone-mineral disorders. The result needs concurrent calcium and relevant clinical information; assay design affects the hormone fragments measured.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Intact PTH is a blood measurement used to assess calcium imbalance, parathyroid function and selected kidney or bone-mineral disorders. The result needs concurrent calcium and relevant clinical information; assay design affects the hormone fragments measured. Sample handling also matters. QXL must confirm its platform, specimen requirements and transport conditions before clinic or eligible home collection.\n\n**Also known as:** iPTH, Parathormone\n\n## What is the Intact Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) test used for?\n\n- Investigation of hypercalcaemia or hypocalcaemia.\n- Clinician-directed assessment of suspected parathyroid dysfunction.\n- Selected monitoring of CKD-related mineral and bone disorders.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Hyperparathyroidism, Hypoparathyroidism\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Intact PTH:** Parathyroid hormone measured by an immunoassay; many intact assays also detect some hormone fragments.\n- **Calcium and renal context:** Concurrent calcium, kidney function, vitamin D and phosphate information may be needed; these are separate tests unless ordered as a panel.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nGeneral principle: a two-site sandwich immunoassay captures different regions of PTH. Second-generation intact assays and third-generation whole/bio-intact assays do not measure exactly the same molecular forms. QXL must identify its assay and validated sample requirements.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood for the assay-approved serum or plasma specimen. PTH stability is handling dependent; QXL must specify prompt processing, temperature and transport instructions for its platform.\n\n- Ask whether a concurrent calcium sample or other bone-mineral tests are required.\n- Tell the laboratory about biotin supplements, vitamin D/calcium treatment and relevant medicines. Do not alter prescribed therapy without medical direction.\n- Confirm any fasting or time-of-day instructions for the local protocol; do not assume every PTH assay has identical preparation.\n- Use a collection service able to meet the validated processing and transport requirements.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation. |\n| Container | Pending QXL assay/SOP confirmation; use the approved collection kit. |\n| Stability | Pending QXL validated time/temperature and transport instructions. |\n| Rejection | QXL to confirm assay-specific rejection rules; identifier mismatch, unsuitable container or compromised sample requires laboratory review. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- High calcium with a PTH that is not appropriately suppressed can suggest PTH-dependent hypercalcaemia; the clinician evaluates the full pattern.\n- Raised PTH can also occur with CKD, vitamin D deficiency or other secondary causes.\n- A PTH result must not be judged against calcium-independent internet cut-offs or a different assay’s reference range.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the QXL report’s assay-specific units, reference interval or decision limit. Age, sex, pregnancy, sampling time and clinical context may change interpretation; there is no universal range for this page.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Assay generation and fragment detection affect results, particularly in CKD.\n- Biotin and other immunoassay interference are method dependent.\n- Delayed processing or unsuitable storage can distort the measured value.\n- PTH alone cannot localise an abnormal parathyroid gland or diagnose every form of bone disease.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Intact PTH and whole/bio-intact PTH have different assay specificity. PTH-related peptide is a separate investigation.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nProvide the diagnostic question, prior results and relevant treatment. Confirm the exact assay, specimen SOP and performing laboratory. Discuss results that conflict with the clinical picture before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Price requires QXL approval; no unverified price is offered.\n- **Report time:** QXL test-specific turnaround pending confirmation; do not infer from another laboratory.\n- **Home collection:** QXL to confirm test-specific eligibility, collection setting and transport before booking. Ask QXL whether eligible home collection in your Bengaluru pincode is free; do not assume every specimen can be collected at home.\n- **Processing:** QXL to confirm in-house versus referral processing and assay/platform.\n- **Report review:** Proposed reviewer assignment only. Medical review, operations verification and sign-off are pending.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why is PTH tested with calcium?\n\nPTH regulates calcium balance. A result can be appropriate or inappropriate depending on the calcium level at the same time.\n\n### Does high PTH always mean a parathyroid tumour?\n\nNo. Vitamin D deficiency, chronic kidney disease and other causes can raise PTH. The clinician assesses the biochemical pattern.\n\n### What does intact mean?\n\nIt describes an assay design aimed at full-length PTH, although many intact assays also detect certain fragments. The assay generation matters.\n\n### Can I compare results from different laboratories?\n\nNot without checking methods, units and reference intervals. Different PTH assays can give different results, especially in kidney disease.\n\n### Do I need to stop biotin?\n\nTell QXL and the treating clinician the dose and last use. Any withholding instruction must be specific to the selected assay and your clinical needs.\n\n### Is fasting required?\n\nConfirm the local protocol and any concurrent tests. Fasting or collection timing may be standardised for comparability rather than applied universally.\n\n### Is ordinary home collection suitable?\n\nOnly if collection and transport meet QXL’s validated PTH stability requirements. Confirm eligibility before booking.\n\n### Can PTH locate a diseased gland?\n\nNo. It is a blood measurement of function. Imaging, when appropriate, addresses a different question after clinical and biochemical assessment.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Anti-TPO Antibody Test in Bengaluru](/blog/anti-tpo-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Clinical principles are supported by the cited textbook sections and current primary guidance; actual QXL kit, specimen volume, stability, price, turnaround and availability remain unverified.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
