    "excerpt": "C-peptide is released when the pancreas makes insulin and can help assess the body’s own insulin production in selected clinical situations. It is not a routine diabetes screening test.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** C-peptide is released when the pancreas makes insulin and can help assess the body’s own insulin production in selected clinical situations. It is not a routine diabetes screening test. Interpretation needs the glucose level, sampling conditions and kidney function. Blood testing commonly uses immunoassay; QXL must confirm its method, collection requirements and report time before an appointment in Bengaluru.\n\n**Also known as:** C peptide, connecting peptide, endogenous insulin secretion\n\n## What is the C-Peptide test used for?\n\n- Clarifying diabetes classification when the clinical picture remains ambiguous.\n- Specialist evaluation of documented hypoglycaemia with glucose, insulin and other indicated investigations.\n- Selected assessment of residual insulin secretion or follow-up after pancreatic intervention.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Insulin secretion disorders\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **C-peptide:** Marker of endogenous insulin secretion, interpreted with concurrent glucose and clinical context.\n- **Concurrent glucose, if ordered:** Shows whether insulin secretion is appropriate for the prevailing glucose; confirm separate inclusion.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nAntibody-based immunoassay commonly measures circulating C-peptide. The assay does not measure injected insulin itself. Platform calibration, cross-reactivity and sample requirements vary; QXL must confirm the actual assay and whether concurrent glucose is included.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum or plasma suitability must be confirmed for the actual QXL assay.\n\n- The clinician must specify fasting, random or stimulated sampling; these are different protocols.\n- Arrange simultaneous glucose when requested and document the last meal and diabetes medicine timing.\n- Do not withhold insulin or deliberately induce a low glucose level at home; supervised hypoglycaemia testing requires a clinical setting.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Confirm minimum and paediatric volume with QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the collection tube or container specified by the actual QXL test SOP. |\n| Stability | Confirm separation deadline, validated transport temperature, cold-chain and stability window with QXL. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, specimen quality, quantity and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A result may indicate endogenous secretion only in relation to the glucose stimulus and clinical setting.\n- A lower result during hyperglycaemia may support insulin deficiency; it does not alone establish a diabetes subtype.\n- Kidney impairment can increase circulating C-peptide and complicate interpretation.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the method, units and context-specific interval or decision limits on the actual laboratory report; no universal numeric normal range is substituted.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Recent food, current glucose, duration of diabetes and renal function influence the result.\n- Assays differ; results and cut-offs from separate platforms may not be directly interchangeable.\n- Insulin-secretagogue exposure can cause an endogenous secretion pattern; C-peptide alone cannot identify its cause.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- C-peptide reflects endogenous secretion, whereas insulin assays may detect endogenous insulin and some injected analogues depending on the method.\n- Glucose and HbA1c remain established tools for diabetes diagnosis; C-peptide addresses a different question.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nState the clinical question, relevant treatment and specimen timing. Discuss unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote, included components and any collection charges.\n- **Report time:** Confirm test-specific QXL reporting time, its starting point, working-day conditions and any referral transit before collection.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL to confirm Bengaluru pincode eligibility, test suitability, collection timing and whether the visit is free.\n- **Processing:** QXL verification pending: performing laboratory, platform and in-house versus referral status.\n- **Report review:** Appropriately qualified laboratory authorisation is required. Ask QXL whether a separate report-discussion appointment is available. This content has not received medical sign-off.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does C-peptide measure the insulin I inject?\n\nNo. Injected insulin does not contain C-peptide; the test assesses secretion from your own pancreas.\n\n### Can this diagnose diabetes by itself?\n\nNo. Diabetes diagnosis uses validated glucose or HbA1c criteria in the appropriate clinical pathway.\n\n### Should I fast for C-peptide?\n\nYour clinician must specify a fasting, random or stimulated protocol. Do not substitute one protocol for another.\n\n### Why is glucose collected at the same time?\n\nC-peptide must be interpreted against the prevailing glucose to assess whether insulin secretion is appropriate.\n\n### Can kidney disease alter C-peptide?\n\nYes. Reduced renal clearance can raise the level and limit interpretation.\n\n### Should I stop insulin before collection?\n\nDo not stop insulin yourself. Obtain a safe, individual medicine plan from your treating clinician.\n\n### Does a low value prove type 1 diabetes?\n\nNo. Disease duration, glucose, kidney function and other clinical or antibody findings may be needed.\n\n### How do I confirm QXL collection and reporting?\n\nAsk QXL for the correct protocol, specimen instructions, concurrent glucose inclusion, current quote and reporting time.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Fasting Insulin & HOMA-IR Test in Bengaluru](/blog/fasting-insulin-homa-ir-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz ninth edition, PDF pages 1734–1739: appropriate C-peptide use, endogenous secretion and assay variation.\n- A Manual of Laboratory and Diagnostic Tests ninth edition, PDF pages 364–365: C-peptide and concurrent glucose context. Current laboratory SOP must govern collection.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
