    "excerpt": "Fasting insulin measures circulating insulin, while HOMA-IR combines fasting insulin and glucose to estimate insulin resistance indirectly. This calculation is mainly a research or selected specialist tool, not a stand-alone diabetes diagnosis or routine population screen.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Fasting insulin measures circulating insulin, while HOMA-IR combines fasting insulin and glucose to estimate insulin resistance indirectly. This calculation is mainly a research or selected specialist tool, not a stand-alone diabetes diagnosis or routine population screen. Assay differences and the person’s metabolic state matter. QXL must confirm the fasting protocol, included glucose measurement, calculation and report time.\n\n**Also known as:** fasting insulin, HOMA1-IR, homeostasis model assessment, insulin resistance estimate\n\n## What is the Fasting Insulin / HOMA-IR test used for?\n\n- Selected specialist assessment when an insulin measurement answers a defined metabolic question.\n- Research or locally validated pathways that explicitly require a fasting insulin/glucose index.\n- Evaluation of hypoglycaemia may use insulin, but requires a different supervised sampling and interpretation protocol.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Insulin resistance\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **Fasting insulin:** Hormone concentration influenced by secretion, clearance, medicines and assay specificity.\n- **Fasting plasma glucose:** Glucose concentration required from the same fasting assessment for HOMA1-IR.\n- **HOMA1-IR calculation:** An indirect model-derived estimate; not a direct measurement of tissue insulin action.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nInsulin is commonly measured by immunoassay; glucose uses a validated chemistry method. HOMA1-IR = fasting insulin (µU/mL) × fasting glucose (mmol/L) / 22.5, or × glucose (mg/dL) / 405. HOMA2 is a different model and must not be labelled as this simple formula. Confirm which assay and calculation QXL actually reports.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum or plasma suitability must be confirmed for the actual QXL assay.\n\n- Arrange the fasting interval specified by QXL and your clinician; both measurements must represent the same fasting state.\n- Record insulin use, glucose-lowering medicines, acute illness and last food intake; obtain an individual medicine plan.\n- Do not extend fasting or omit treatment yourself, especially if pregnant, unwell or at risk of hypoglycaemia.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Confirm minimum and paediatric volume with QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the collection tube or container specified by the actual QXL test SOP. |\n| Stability | Confirm separation deadline, validated transport temperature, cold-chain and stability window with QXL. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, specimen quality, quantity and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- Higher HOMA1-IR may reflect lower insulin sensitivity in an appropriate study or validated context; no universal clinical threshold applies.\n- Insulin secretion, clearance, glycaemic state and assay calibration affect the index.\n- Glucose and HbA1c, blood pressure, adiposity and lipids often provide more useful routine risk information.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the method, units and context-specific interval or decision limits on the actual laboratory report; no universal numeric normal range is substituted.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Routine insulin testing for most people with diabetes or cardiovascular risk is not recommended by laboratory diabetes guidance.\n- Injected insulin, severe insulin deficiency, unstable glucose or acute illness may undermine the model’s assumptions.\n- An index cannot establish diabetes, PCOS or a specific tissue-level defect.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- HOMA-IR is an indirect fasting estimate; a hyperinsulinaemic euglycaemic clamp is a specialist research measurement.\n- HbA1c, fasting plasma glucose and an indicated oral glucose tolerance test answer established dysglycaemia questions.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nState the clinical question, relevant treatment and specimen timing. Discuss unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote, included components and any collection charges.\n- **Report time:** Confirm test-specific QXL reporting time, its starting point, working-day conditions and any referral transit before collection.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL to confirm Bengaluru pincode eligibility, test suitability, collection timing and whether the visit is free.\n- **Processing:** QXL verification pending: performing laboratory, platform and in-house versus referral status.\n- **Report review:** Appropriately qualified laboratory authorisation is required. Ask QXL whether a separate report-discussion appointment is available. This content has not received medical sign-off.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is HOMA-IR a diabetes diagnostic test?\n\nNo. It estimates a component of insulin resistance and does not replace established glucose or HbA1c pathways.\n\n### How is HOMA1-IR calculated?\n\nUse fasting insulin in µU/mL multiplied by fasting glucose in mmol/L, divided by 22.5; with glucose in mg/dL divide by 405.\n\n### Is HOMA2 the same calculation?\n\nNo. HOMA2 is a different computer model; reports must identify which model and inputs were used.\n\n### What HOMA-IR value is normal?\n\nThere is no universal threshold across assays, ages and populations. Use a clinically justified, locally validated interpretation.\n\n### Can I calculate it from a random glucose?\n\nNo. The simple HOMA1 model requires fasting insulin and fasting glucose from the same fasting assessment.\n\n### Can I use this while injecting insulin?\n\nInjected insulin can affect assay results and model validity. Discuss a suitable assessment with your clinician.\n\n### Should everyone with PCOS request fasting insulin?\n\nNo. Routine insulin testing is not recommended simply because PCOS is suspected; the clinician should define the question.\n\n### What should I confirm before booking?\n\nConfirm fasting instructions, medicine safety, glucose inclusion, model name, current price and test-specific QXL reporting time.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [C-Peptide Test in Bengaluru](/blog/c-peptide-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz ninth edition, PDF pages 1734–1736 and 1744: limited routine insulin utility and indirect HOMA assessment.\n- Diabetes Care laboratory recommendations (2023) cross-check routine-use limits; Wallace et al. (2004) provides HOMA1 units and equation.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
