    "excerpt": "DHEA-S is an androgen precursor produced mainly by the adrenal glands. Measuring it can contribute to assessment of androgen excess, unusual pubertal development or selected adrenal disorders.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** DHEA-S is an androgen precursor produced mainly by the adrenal glands. Measuring it can contribute to assessment of androgen excess, unusual pubertal development or selected adrenal disorders. It is not an anti-ageing or general wellness score. Age, sex and other hormone results are essential to interpretation. QXL must confirm its assay, sample instructions and reporting time before collection.\n\n**Also known as:** DHEAS, DHEA sulfate, adrenal androgen, dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate\n\n## What is the DHEA-S (Dehydroepiandrosterone Sulfate) test used for?\n\n- Hirsutism, virilisation or other signs of androgen excess requiring clinical assessment.\n- Selected investigation of premature or delayed pubertal development.\n- Specialist adrenal evaluation alongside targeted hormones and imaging when indicated.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Adrenal androgen excess\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **DHEA-S:** Adrenal androgen precursor used with other hormones to assess the source and pattern of androgen production.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nDHEA-S is commonly measured by immunoassay; some laboratories use mass-spectrometric steroid methods. Antibody cross-reactivity and other assay interferences can matter. QXL must identify its validated platform and reference intervals rather than assume a generic methodology.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum or plasma suitability must be confirmed for the actual QXL assay.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary for DHEA-S alone; confirm instructions for any combined hormone panel.\n- Disclose DHEA products, hormonal medicines, steroids and biotin-containing supplements.\n- Do not stop prescribed hormones or steroids without advice; provide age and relevant pubertal or menstrual history.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Confirm minimum and paediatric volume with QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the collection tube or container specified by the actual QXL test SOP. |\n| Stability | Confirm separation deadline, validated transport temperature, cold-chain and stability window with QXL. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, specimen quality, quantity and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A higher result may support an adrenal contribution to androgen excess, but does not by itself identify a tumour.\n- DHEA-S changes substantially with age; results require age- and context-appropriate intervals.\n- A lower value is nonspecific and cannot alone diagnose adrenal insufficiency.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the method, units and context-specific interval or decision limits on the actual laboratory report; no universal numeric normal range is substituted.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- DHEA-S is not the same analyte as unconjugated DHEA.\n- Immunoassay interference and cross-reacting steroids can cause unexpected results.\n- A single value cannot diagnose PCOS, congenital adrenal hyperplasia or an adrenal tumour.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- Testosterone, androstenedione and selected 17-hydroxyprogesterone testing may help define an androgen-excess pathway.\n- Cortisol/ACTH and appropriate stimulation tests address adrenal cortisol function; DHEA-S cannot replace them.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nState the clinical question, relevant treatment and specimen timing. Discuss unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote, included components and any collection charges.\n- **Report time:** Confirm test-specific QXL reporting time, its starting point, working-day conditions and any referral transit before collection.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL to confirm Bengaluru pincode eligibility, test suitability, collection timing and whether the visit is free.\n- **Processing:** QXL verification pending: performing laboratory, platform and in-house versus referral status.\n- **Report review:** Appropriately qualified laboratory authorisation is required. Ask QXL whether a separate report-discussion appointment is available. This content has not received medical sign-off.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is DHEA-S found only in men?\n\nNo. It is present in people of all sexes and is produced mainly by the adrenal glands.\n\n### Is DHEA-S the same as DHEA?\n\nNo. DHEA-S is the sulfated form; the assays and reference intervals differ.\n\n### Can this explain excess facial hair?\n\nIt may contribute to an androgen-excess assessment alongside history, examination and other hormone tests.\n\n### Does high DHEA-S prove an adrenal tumour?\n\nNo. Several conditions can raise it; the magnitude, other findings and specialist assessment determine next steps.\n\n### Do I need to fast?\n\nUsually not for DHEA-S alone, but follow instructions for other tests collected at the same visit.\n\n### Should I disclose DHEA supplements?\n\nYes. DHEA products and other hormones can change the result; do not alter prescribed treatment yourself.\n\n### Can I use DHEA-S to choose anti-ageing supplements?\n\nNo. A result does not establish benefit or safety of DHEA supplementation.\n\n### Why is there no single normal DHEA-S range here?\n\nAge, sex and method influence the interval. Use the actual QXL report and clinician interpretation.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [Total & Free Testosterone Test in Bengaluru](/blog/testosterone-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [Cortisol Test in Bengaluru](/blog/cortisol-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz ninth edition, PDF pages 2232–2235: adrenal androgen physiology; PDF pages 2270–2271: assay principles and interference.\n- MedlinePlus DHEA sulfate guidance cross-checks indications, preparation and the need for age/sex context.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
