    "excerpt": "CA 19-9 is a blood tumour marker used mainly in the assessment and monitoring of selected pancreatic and biliary cancers. It is unsuitable for general cancer screening.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** CA 19-9 is a blood tumour marker used mainly in the assessment and monitoring of selected pancreatic and biliary cancers. It is unsuitable for general cancer screening. Benign biliary obstruction and inflammation can raise it, while some people do not produce measurable CA 19-9. QXL must verify its assay, serial-testing comparability and reporting time before booking.\n\n**Also known as:** CA19-9, carbohydrate antigen 19-9, cancer antigen 19-9, pancreatobiliary tumour marker\n\n## What is the CA 19-9 test used for?\n\n- Baseline and follow-up assessment in a clinician-directed pancreatic or biliary cancer pathway.\n- Monitoring response or suspected progression when the tumour is known to produce CA 19-9.\n- An adjunct in symptomatic investigation when the clinician has already identified a relevant suspicion.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Pancreatic cancer\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **CA 19-9:** A tumour-associated carbohydrate antigen; changes may inform selected cancer monitoring with imaging and clinical assessment.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nCA 19-9 is commonly quantified by immunoassay. Results from different manufacturers are not necessarily interchangeable; serial results should ideally use the same validated method. QXL must confirm its assay and any method changes.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum or plasma suitability must be confirmed for the actual QXL assay.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary for CA 19-9 alone; confirm requirements for combined tests.\n- Tell the clinician about jaundice, biliary obstruction, recent drainage and pancreatic or liver inflammation.\n- Provide previous marker reports, treatment dates and biotin/supplement history for serial comparison.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Confirm minimum and paediatric volume with QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the collection tube or container specified by the actual QXL test SOP. |\n| Stability | Confirm separation deadline, validated transport temperature, cold-chain and stability window with QXL. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, specimen quality, quantity and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A rising or falling trend can inform assessment only alongside imaging, symptoms and the treatment timeline.\n- Biliary obstruction, cholangitis, pancreatitis and liver disease can increase CA 19-9 without cancer.\n- Some individuals produce little or no CA 19-9; a low result cannot exclude malignancy.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the method, units and context-specific interval or decision limits on the actual laboratory report; no universal numeric normal range is substituted.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- CA 19-9 cannot independently diagnose pancreatic cancer or locate a tumour.\n- It is not an appropriate general population screening test or a replacement for imaging and tissue assessment.\n- Assay changes, inflammation and biliary obstruction can mimic a change in tumour-related production.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- CA 19-9 complements appropriate imaging and pathological evaluation; CEA and other markers are not interchangeable substitutes.\n- Bilirubin and the clinical status of biliary obstruction help contextualise a result.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nState the clinical question, relevant treatment and specimen timing. Discuss unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote, included components and any collection charges.\n- **Report time:** Confirm test-specific QXL reporting time, its starting point, working-day conditions and any referral transit before collection.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL to confirm Bengaluru pincode eligibility, test suitability, collection timing and whether the visit is free.\n- **Processing:** QXL verification pending: performing laboratory, platform and in-house versus referral status.\n- **Report review:** Appropriately qualified laboratory authorisation is required. Ask QXL whether a separate report-discussion appointment is available. This content has not received medical sign-off.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Can CA 19-9 screen me for pancreatic cancer?\n\nIt is unsuitable for general screening because benign conditions can raise it and some cancers do not raise it.\n\n### Does a high result mean cancer?\n\nNo. Biliary blockage, inflammation and other noncancerous conditions can increase the level.\n\n### Can a normal result exclude cancer?\n\nNo. Some tumours and some individuals do not produce measurable CA 19-9.\n\n### Why does jaundice matter?\n\nBiliary obstruction can increase CA 19-9. Your clinician may reassess after appropriate treatment of the obstruction.\n\n### Why should follow-up use the same laboratory method?\n\nMethods can give different values; consistent testing improves interpretation of trends.\n\n### Do I need to fast for CA 19-9?\n\nUsually not for the marker alone. Confirm preparation if other investigations are ordered.\n\n### Can a falling result prove treatment has worked?\n\nIt may support a response assessment, but imaging and the wider clinical evaluation remain necessary.\n\n### What details should I bring to QXL?\n\nBring the request, prior marker reports, treatment timeline and information about jaundice; confirm the quote and reporting time.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [CEA Test in Bengaluru](/blog/cea-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [AFP Test in Bengaluru](/blog/afp-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz ninth edition, PDF pages 1105–1106: CA 19-9 clinical applications, non-producers, obstruction and serial method consistency. PDF pages 1069–1070 list benign causes.\n- MedlinePlus CA 19-9 guidance cross-checks screening limits and clinical interpretation.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
