    "excerpt": "CEA is a blood tumour marker most often used in the management of colorectal cancer and selected other cancers. Serial results can support treatment assessment or follow-up, but CEA cannot screen for or diagnose cancer by itself.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** CEA is a blood tumour marker most often used in the management of colorectal cancer and selected other cancers. Serial results can support treatment assessment or follow-up, but CEA cannot screen for or diagnose cancer by itself. Smoking and benign disease may raise the level. QXL must confirm its assay and reporting time and support comparison with prior reports.\n\n**Also known as:** CEA, carcinoembryonic antigen, colorectal tumour marker\n\n## What is the CEA (Carcinoembryonic Antigen) test used for?\n\n- A clinician-directed baseline assessment before treatment for a relevant diagnosed cancer.\n- Monitoring selected cancers known to produce CEA during treatment.\n- Follow-up for possible recurrence in an agreed oncology surveillance pathway.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Colorectal cancer\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **CEA:** An oncofetal antigen used as an adjunct for monitoring selected cancers; it is not cancer-specific.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nCEA is commonly measured using an antibody-based immunoassay. Platform-dependent differences and antibody interference can affect results. QXL must confirm the method used and identify any change that could affect longitudinal comparison.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous blood; serum or plasma suitability must be confirmed for the actual QXL assay.\n\n- Special fasting preparation is generally unnecessary for a CEA blood sample; check combined-test instructions.\n- Disclose smoking status, liver disease, inflammatory illness and current cancer treatment.\n- Bring previous CEA reports and treatment dates; do not change medicines or treatment in response to a marker alone.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Confirm minimum and paediatric volume with QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the collection tube or container specified by the actual QXL test SOP. |\n| Stability | Confirm separation deadline, validated transport temperature, cold-chain and stability window with QXL. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, specimen quality, quantity and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- Trends may support response or recurrence assessment when the cancer is known to produce CEA.\n- Smoking, liver dysfunction and other benign conditions can cause increased values.\n- A normal concentration does not exclude colorectal cancer or recurrence.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the method, units and context-specific interval or decision limits on the actual laboratory report; no universal numeric normal range is substituted.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Low sensitivity for early disease makes CEA unsuitable for general colorectal cancer screening.\n- CEA cannot distinguish the primary tumour site or diagnose a recurrence without further assessment.\n- Unexpected trends need review for biological, clinical and method-related explanations.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- FIT and indicated colonoscopy belong to colorectal screening/diagnostic pathways; CEA does not replace them.\n- CEA and CA 19-9 have different clinical roles and are not interchangeable cancer scores.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nState the clinical question, relevant treatment and specimen timing. Discuss unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote, included components and any collection charges.\n- **Report time:** Confirm test-specific QXL reporting time, its starting point, working-day conditions and any referral transit before collection.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL to confirm Bengaluru pincode eligibility, test suitability, collection timing and whether the visit is free.\n- **Processing:** QXL verification pending: performing laboratory, platform and in-house versus referral status.\n- **Report review:** Appropriately qualified laboratory authorisation is required. Ask QXL whether a separate report-discussion appointment is available. This content has not received medical sign-off.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is CEA a bowel cancer screening test?\n\nNo. Appropriate stool testing and colonoscopy pathways address screening; CEA is mainly a management marker.\n\n### Can smoking increase CEA?\n\nYes. Smoking status is relevant to interpretation and should be recorded.\n\n### Does elevated CEA prove a cancer has returned?\n\nNo. A change may prompt further evaluation but does not alone establish recurrence.\n\n### Can CEA be normal in colorectal cancer?\n\nYes. Not all cancers produce a raised result, particularly early disease.\n\n### Why are several CEA measurements sometimes requested?\n\nA trend is often more informative than a single result when the clinician has defined the monitoring purpose.\n\n### Should I fast before the blood test?\n\nUsually not for CEA alone; follow the instructions for any combined investigations.\n\n### Can results from different methods be compared directly?\n\nMethod differences may affect values. Discuss a method change with the laboratory before interpreting a trend.\n\n### What should I confirm with QXL?\n\nConfirm the assay, current quote, reporting time and arrangements for comparing prior oncology reports.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [CA 19-9 Test in Bengaluru](/blog/ca19-9-test-bengaluru-guide)\n- [AFP Test in Bengaluru](/blog/afp-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Tietz ninth edition, PDF pages 1083–1084: CEA in colorectal cancer, insufficient early-disease sensitivity and benign confounders. PDF pages 1069–1070 list nonmalignant elevations.\n- MedlinePlus CEA guidance cross-checks monitoring use, smoking and preparation.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
