    "excerpt": "TB molecular testing looks for genetic material from Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex in an appropriate specimen. Certain cartridge assays also assess rifampicin-resistance-associated targets; a generic PCR request does not guarantee this component.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** TB molecular testing looks for genetic material from Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex in an appropriate specimen. Certain cartridge assays also assess rifampicin-resistance-associated targets; a generic PCR request does not guarantee this component. This supports evaluation of active tuberculosis, not latent infection screening. QXL must confirm the assay, accepted specimen, resistance targets, referral status and reporting time before collection.\n\n**Also known as:** tuberculosis NAAT, MTB PCR, CBNAAT, Xpert MTB/RIF, cartridge based nucleic acid amplification\n\n## What is the TB PCR / CBNAAT test used for?\n\n- Suspected pulmonary TB requiring a clinician-directed respiratory specimen investigation.\n- Selected extrapulmonary TB investigation when the assay is validated for the requested specimen.\n- Rapid molecular resistance assessment when explicitly included in the chosen assay and care pathway.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Active tuberculosis\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **M. tuberculosis complex nucleic acid:** Direct molecular evidence from a validated clinical specimen; not an antibody or immune-response measurement.\n- **Rifampicin-resistance-associated target, if included:** Available only with specified assays; the report and follow-up pathway must identify the resistance assessment performed.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nA validated NAAT amplifies M. tuberculosis complex nucleic acid. CBNAAT describes cartridge-based testing; Xpert MTB/RIF or Ultra have assay-specific targets and reporting rules. A generic TB PCR may differ. QXL must confirm assay identity, validated specimen types and whether rifampicin resistance is assessed; PCR does not replace all culture or susceptibility investigations.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Site-appropriate specimen: commonly sputum for pulmonary TB; other material requires assay validation and QXL acceptance before collection.\n\n- Obtain test-specific collection instructions before producing a sample; sputum is lower-respiratory material rather than saliva.\n- Arrange sputum collection using the designated infection-control setting and container; do not assume an ordinary home phlebotomy visit is suitable.\n- Tell the clinician about previous TB, current treatment and specimen site. Invasive specimens must be collected by the appropriate clinical team.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Confirm minimum and paediatric volume with QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the collection tube or container specified by the actual QXL test SOP. |\n| Stability | Confirm separation deadline, validated transport temperature, cold-chain and stability window with QXL. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, specimen quality, quantity and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- A detected result supports active TB evaluation in the clinical context; nucleic acid can persist after organisms are no longer viable.\n- A negative result does not exclude TB, particularly with paucibacillary or extrapulmonary disease.\n- Resistance results require prompt specialist follow-up and any indicated confirmatory or additional susceptibility testing.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the method, units and context-specific interval or decision limits on the actual laboratory report; no universal numeric normal range is substituted.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- Different NAAT platforms and specimen types have different performance and resistance capabilities.\n- Sampling quality, low organism burden and inhibitors can affect detection.\n- This is not an IGRA or skin test for TB infection and should not be used as a latent-TB screen.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- AFB smear, culture and drug-susceptibility testing provide complementary information; the clinician should arrange the relevant pathway.\n- IGRA/TST assess immune sensitisation to TB infection; they do not replace direct organism detection for suspected active disease.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nState the clinical question, relevant treatment and specimen timing. Discuss unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote, included components and any collection charges.\n- **Report time:** Confirm test-specific QXL reporting time, its starting point, working-day conditions and any referral transit before collection.\n- **Home collection:** Specimen collection and infection-control requirements depend on the site of suspected TB. Confirm the QXL kit and safe submission pathway; invasive samples require a clinical facility.\n- **Processing:** QXL verification pending: performing laboratory, platform and in-house versus referral status.\n- **Report review:** Appropriately qualified laboratory authorisation is required. Ask QXL whether a separate report-discussion appointment is available. This content has not received medical sign-off.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is CBNAAT the same as every TB PCR?\n\nNo. CBNAAT is cartridge-based NAAT; assay targets, specimen validation and resistance reporting differ from other PCR tests.\n\n### Does this test detect latent TB?\n\nIt is used for direct organism detection in suspected active disease. Latent infection assessment uses a separate clinical pathway, often IGRA or TST.\n\n### Does a negative result rule out TB?\n\nNo. Further samples, culture, imaging or other investigations may be needed if suspicion remains.\n\n### Will rifampicin resistance always be reported?\n\nOnly if that target is included in the specified assay. Confirm the exact test before booking.\n\n### Can I submit saliva instead of sputum?\n\nSaliva is not equivalent to a good lower-respiratory sputum specimen. Follow the collection instructions.\n\n### Can PCR stay positive after treatment?\n\nYes. Nucleic acid from nonviable organisms can persist, so the result must be interpreted with treatment history.\n\n### Do I still need culture or other susceptibility tests?\n\nOften they provide essential complementary information. Follow the TB clinician’s diagnostic and resistance-testing plan.\n\n### Can this be collected through a routine home blood visit?\n\nDo not assume so. Confirm specimen type, infection-control arrangements, safe transport, current quote and reporting time with QXL.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [HIV 4th Generation Test in Bengaluru](/blog/hiv-1-2-antigen-antibody-4th-generation-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Henry’s Clinical Diagnosis and Management by Laboratory Methods, 24th edition (2022), PDF pages 1364 and 1366: direct NAAT, cartridge testing and resistance considerations.\n- CDC molecular testing material cross-checks clinical/culture correlation and negative-NAAT limits. WHO issued a second-edition diagnosis update on 3 November 2026; new diagnostic classes and sample options require current Indian/local pathway and assay verification. No new specimen is assumed available at QXL.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
