    "excerpt": "Haemoglobin fractionation separates and estimates haemoglobin types to investigate selected inherited haemoglobin disorders, including thalassaemia and sickle variants. HPLC and electrophoresis are related investigative approaches with different separation principles.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Haemoglobin fractionation separates and estimates haemoglobin types to investigate selected inherited haemoglobin disorders, including thalassaemia and sickle variants. HPLC and electrophoresis are related investigative approaches with different separation principles. Results need the blood count, iron status, age and transfusion history; some patterns require confirmation. QXL must confirm its method, reporting scope and turnaround before collection in Bengaluru.\n\n**Also known as:** haemoglobin electrophoresis, Hb HPLC, haemoglobin fractionation, thalassaemia screen, sickle haemoglobin assessment\n\n## What is the Hemoglobin Electrophoresis / HPLC test used for?\n\n- Unexplained microcytosis or anaemia when a haemoglobinopathy is suspected.\n- Carrier assessment in an appropriate preconception, antenatal or family-history pathway.\n- Evaluation of a suspected sickle or other structural haemoglobin variant.\n\n**Conditions it helps investigate:** Thalassaemia, Haemoglobin variants\n\n## What does the test measure?\n\n- **HbA, HbA2 and HbF, as resolved by the method:** Fractions that help assess haemoglobin production patterns in age-appropriate context.\n- **Variant haemoglobin peaks or bands:** Presumptive findings may require an alternative method, family studies or molecular confirmation.\n\n## How is it tested?\n\nHPLC separates haemoglobin fractions chromatographically; electrophoresis separates them by migration in an electric field. They are not identical methods. Peaks or bands are interpreted with other haematology data, and an alternative method or molecular test may be required. QXL must confirm the platform, HbA2/HbF quantification and confirmatory workflow offered.\n\n## Sample and preparation\n\n**Specimen:** Venous whole blood, commonly EDTA-anticoagulated; QXL must confirm the validated tube, minimum volume and transport window.\n\n- Fasting is generally unnecessary for haemoglobin fractionation alone; confirm any combined-test instructions.\n- Provide age, family history, pregnancy status, iron treatment and all recent transfusion dates.\n- Tell QXL about known haemoglobin variants, stem-cell transplantation or relevant prior reports before collection.\n\n| Specimen detail | What to confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | Confirm minimum and paediatric volume with QXL test master. |\n| Container | Use the collection tube or container specified by the actual QXL test SOP. |\n| Stability | Confirm separation deadline, validated transport temperature, cold-chain and stability window with QXL. |\n| Rejection | QXL must confirm identification, specimen quality, quantity and transport acceptance criteria. |\n\n## How are the results interpreted?\n\n- HbA2, HbF and variant proportions must be interpreted with age, red-cell indices and iron status.\n- A carrier state and a clinically significant disorder can have different patterns; counselling may be needed.\n- Recent transfusion can introduce donor haemoglobin and obscure the person’s underlying pattern.\n\n**Reference interval:** Use the method, units and context-specific interval or decision limits on the actual laboratory report; no universal numeric normal range is substituted.\n\n## What are the limitations?\n\n- A normal fractionation result does not exclude all alpha-thalassaemia or rare/unstable haemoglobin variants.\n- Variants can co-elute or co-migrate; a peak or band is not always a definitive molecular identification.\n- Iron deficiency and transfusion can complicate carrier interpretation; repeat or additional testing may be appropriate.\n\n## How does it compare with other tests?\n\n- CBC, blood film and iron studies complement fractionation; they cannot independently identify all haemoglobin variants.\n- Molecular testing may be required for definitive variant identification or reproductive-risk assessment.\n\n## Notes for referring clinicians\n\nState the clinical question, relevant treatment and specimen timing. Discuss unexpected or discordant results with the laboratory before changing management.\n\n## Price, report time and home collection\n\n- **Price:** Request the current QXL quote, included components and any collection charges.\n- **Report time:** Confirm test-specific QXL reporting time, its starting point, working-day conditions and any referral transit before collection.\n- **Home collection:** Ask QXL to confirm Bengaluru pincode eligibility, test suitability, collection timing and whether the visit is free.\n- **Processing:** QXL verification pending: performing laboratory, platform and in-house versus referral status.\n- **Report review:** Appropriately qualified laboratory authorisation is required. Ask QXL whether a separate report-discussion appointment is available. This content has not received medical sign-off.\n\nCall [+91 9964 639 639](tel:+919964639639) or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639) to confirm the current quote, preparation and collection slot.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Are HPLC and electrophoresis identical tests?\n\nNo. They separate haemoglobin using different principles; the laboratory should state the method and interpretation used.\n\n### Does a normal result exclude all thalassaemia?\n\nNo. Some alpha-thalassaemia and other conditions can have a normal fractionation pattern.\n\n### Why must I report recent transfusions?\n\nDonor red cells may change the observed fractions and mask the underlying haemoglobin pattern.\n\n### Can iron deficiency affect interpretation?\n\nYes. Iron status can complicate assessment, particularly of carrier patterns, and must be considered.\n\n### Does an abnormal peak prove the exact genetic variant?\n\nNot always. Co-elution or co-migration may require another separation technique or molecular confirmation.\n\n### Is fasting required?\n\nUsually not for fractionation alone; confirm requirements if other investigations are ordered.\n\n### Should my partner be tested if I am a carrier?\n\nA clinician or genetic counsellor may recommend partner testing to assess reproductive risk in the relevant setting.\n\n### What will QXL include in the report?\n\nConfirm the method, HbA2/HbF quantification, interpretation, confirmatory options, current quote and turnaround before booking.\n\n## Related test guides\n\n- [D-Dimer Test in Bengaluru](/blog/d-dimer-test-bengaluru-guide)\n\n## Sources\n\n- Dacie and Lewis Practical Haematology, 12th edition (2017), PDF pages 297–299 (printed pages 289–291): integrated haemoglobinopathy investigation, separation methods and quality requirements.\n- ARUP Consult cross-checks transfusion effects, iron studies and the need for definitive confirmation in selected variants.\n\n*Educational information only; not a diagnosis or a substitute for a doctor’s advice.*",
